Designers who have read Mayer tend to over-apply both: they cue everything that seems important, which destroys the contrast that makes signaling work, and they chop a smooth explanation into so many clicks that the segmenting interrupts comprehension instead of pacing it. Knowing the principle's name is not the skill. The skill is the calibration: how much to cue, where exactly to place a break, and how to tell from learner behavior whether you cued too little or too much.\n\nThis section moves both principles from the textbook definition to a procedure you can run on an actual frame. Signaling is treated as a scarce budget of attention you spend deliberately; segmenting is treated as cutting an explanation at its conceptual seams so the learner controls the pace of essential processing. The defensible decision is not \"I applied signaling,\" it is \"I cued this one element and not those three, and I broke the sequence here and not there, and here is why.\"\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Apply the scarcity and contrast law of signaling: justify cuing exactly one element on a frame and name the cues withheld.\n- Run the seam test to place a learner-controlled segmentation break at a conceptual boundary, distinguishing segmenting from pagination and from auto-advance.\n- Treat a signaling cue as a cognitive-load expenditure and judge an AI-proposed cue against its processing cost for a named learner.\n- Tie cue count and segment count to learner expertise (novice versus near-expert, expertise reversal) and document calibration as restraint in the storyboard trace.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer proudly presents a lab-safety frame: every step heading is bold red, each diagram sits in a yellow box, and two arrows pulse for emphasis. \"I used signaling on everything important,\" she says. But when ten elements are all emphasized, none of them is, because signaling works only by contrast against an unemphasized background. The frame has more cuing and less guidance than the plain version it replaced.\n\nAcross the room, another designer split a single fluid demonstration into eleven Continue clicks, one per sentence, \"to apply segmenting.\" Learners now lose the thread between clicks because the breaks fall inside ideas, not between them. Both designers cited the right principle and produced a worse frame. This section is about the judgment that sits between the principle and the panel.\n\n## Core Concept\n\n**Signaling** (Mayer's cueing principle) means adding non-content cues, contrast, position, arrows, isolation, brief motion, that direct limited attention to the part of the display that matters now. Its mechanism is contrast against a background, so its first law is scarcity: the proportion of the display that is cued must stay small. The practical question is never \"is this important?\" (everything on a teaching frame is) but \"is this where attention must land at this moment in the task?\" That converts signaling from decoration into routing.\n\n**Segmenting** (Mayer's segmenting principle) means breaking a continuous explanation into learner-paced units so that essential processing of one chunk completes before the next begins. Its mechanism is managing intrinsic load over time: a high-element-interactivity process (mitosis, a four-step procedure) overwhelms working memory if delivered as one continuous stream, but becomes tractable in paced units. The skill is locating the conceptual seam, the boundary between one complete idea and the next, and placing the break there, never inside an idea.\n\nThe two principles interact and can collide:\n\n- Over-signaling is a coherence violation in disguise: extra cues become extraneous load, so [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load) predicts harm even when each cue \"looks helpful.\"\n- Over-segmenting fractures a single idea, raising load instead of lowering it; the break must fall at a seam, not at a sentence boundary.\n- UDL learner variability sets the calibration target: novices need more, larger segments and clearer cues, while experts experience the same cues and breaks as redundant interruptions (the expertise reversal effect). A defensible decision names the learner whose pacing it is tuned to.\n\nSignaling works by contrast, so its routing power is zero-sum across the cued elements. Use the explorer to see why one cue routes attention but five cues route nothing: the contrast a learner perceives on the single element that matters is the salience of one cue divided by the number of elements competing for it. Watch how routed attention collapses as you cue more.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Cue budget: contrast versus over-cuing\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"cued\",\"label\":\"Elements cued on the frame\",\"min\":1,\"max\":10,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"elements\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"salience\",\"label\":\"Salience strength per cue\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"level\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"background\",\"label\":\"Quiet background elements\",\"min\":0,\"max\":12,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"elements\",\"default\":8}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Routed attention on the target\",\"unit\":\"index\",\"expr\":\"salience / cued\"},{\"label\":\"Display proportion cued\",\"unit\":\"ratio\",\"expr\":\"cued / (cued + background)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"cued\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nThe first output falls as `cued` rises: cuing the active step alone (cued = 1) keeps routed attention high, while cuing all four step headings (cued = 4) quarters it. The scarcity law is visible in the numbers, not just the slogan.\n\n## Worked Example: Fixing Over-Cuing and Adding Real Segmenting\n\nHere is a single frame from a four-step lab-safety procedure, shown before and after a signaling-and-segmenting revision.\n\n**Before (over-cued, auto-advancing):**\n\n> Frame 2. All four step headings are bold red, each diagram sits in a yellow box, two arrows pulse, and the whole sequence auto-plays end to end with no learner control. Caption: made it pop so students notice the steps.\n\nTwo problems. First, contrast has collapsed: when every step is cued, none reads as the one to attend to now. Second, there is no segmenting; the learner cannot pace the four steps.\n\n**After (one cue, learner-paced segments):**\n\n> Frame 2. Only the step currently in focus is bold (one cue, by contrast). The other three headings are plain gray until reached. A Continue control ends each step, so the learner advances one meaningful unit at a time. Caption: signal the active step; let the learner set the pace.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Signaling fix: reduced from four simultaneous cues to one active-step cue. Warrant: signaling works by contrast, so a single salient element directs limited attention.\n2. Load check: removed the pulsing arrows. The arrow motion competed with reading the step it was meant to mark, spending load without adding guidance.\n3. Segmenting fix: replaced auto-play with a Continue control at each conceptual seam (between steps), not mid-step.\n4. Rejected suggestion: an AI tool proposed color-coding all four steps for consistency. Rejected, because consistent color across all steps reintroduces the over-cuing the revision just removed.\n\nThe improvement is not more polish. It is less cuing used more precisely, plus learner control placed at the conceptual seams.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor signaling, set a cue budget and defend it: on any single frame, cue the one element where attention must land for the current action, and demote everything else. Before adding a cue, ask \"what is currently competing with this for attention, and can I remove that instead of cuing harder?\" Removing a distractor is usually cheaper than adding a signal.\n\nFor segmenting, run the seam test on every break you insert:\n\n1. **Seam test:** Does the break fall between two complete ideas, or inside one? If a learner would be mid-thought at the break, move it.\n2. **Control test:** Does the learner trigger the next segment, or does the system auto-advance? Segmenting requires learner pacing; auto-advance is not segmenting.\n3. **Count test:** Could two adjacent segments be one without overloading the target learner? If yes, merge them; over-segmentation is as real a defect as under-segmentation.\n\nA decision survives only if you can name both the cue you spent and the cue you withheld, and both the seam you cut and the seam you left whole.\n\nWork a live calibration dilemma. A four-stroke-engine animation cues all four strokes in red and auto-plays end to end; novices report they cannot follow it.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Calibrating cues and seams on the engine frame\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Novices cannot tell which stroke the narration is describing. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add a sixth pulsing arrow to the active stroke for extra emphasis\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Adding cues raises the cued count, which lowers contrast further. You spent more budget and routed less attention.\"},{\"label\":\"Cue only the active stroke and gray the other three until reached\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"You restored contrast by withholding three cues. One salient element now routes attention to the active stroke.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Learners are now more lost, not less. What does the contrast law tell you?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Remove cues until only the active stroke is emphasized\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: contrast is zero-sum, so the fix is subtraction, not addition.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Over-cuing destroys the contrast mechanism; more emphasis is not safer.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Contrast is fixed, but learners still cannot re-watch compression. Where do you place the learner-paced break?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Auto-advance every two seconds so pacing is consistent\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Auto-advance is not segmenting: the learner does not control essential processing.\"},{\"label\":\"Put a Continue control at each between-stroke seam, never mid-stroke\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the break falls at the conceptual seam and the learner triggers it.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Consistent timing still overruns slow learners. What is the governing principle?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Hand pacing to the learner at the seams\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Segmenting requires learner-controlled advancement at conceptual seams.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Timed auto-advance manages the clock, not the learner’s processing.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"You cued one element and broke at three seams. What must your decision record name?\",\"feedback\":\"It must name both the cue you spent and the three you withheld, both the seams you cut and the mid-stroke seams you left whole, and the novice learner this calibration targets.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is that more signaling is safer than less. It is not symmetric: under-cuing leaves attention undirected, but over-cuing actively destroys the contrast mechanism, so the over-cued frame can score worse than a frame with no cues at all. A second misreading treats segmenting as \"add a Next button between every screen.\" Pagination is not segmenting unless the page breaks coincide with conceptual seams and the learner controls advancement for the purpose of completing essential processing.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A designer splits a single continuous explanation of one idea into eleven Continue clicks, one per sentence. Why might this lower learning rather than raise it?\" explanation=\"The breaks fall inside one idea instead of between ideas, so the learner is repeatedly interrupted mid-thought. Over-segmenting fractures essential processing and raises load; the break must sit at a conceptual seam, not at every sentence boundary.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It helps, because more learner control is always better\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It hurts, because the breaks fall inside one idea rather than at conceptual seams, fracturing processing\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is neutral, because pagination has no effect on load\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a real frame through both principles. A three-minute animation explains how a four-stroke engine works. The first draft cues all four strokes in red simultaneously and plays end to end with no stops. Apply signaling: cue only the stroke currently in motion (one salient element), and gray the other three until reached, so contrast routes attention to the active stroke. Apply segmenting: find the seams, which here are the boundaries between strokes (intake, compression, power, exhaust), and place a learner-controlled Continue at each of the three seams, not in the middle of a stroke where a piston is mid-travel.\n\nNow predict the failure each fix prevents. Without the signaling fix, a learner watching four red strokes at once cannot tell which motion the narration is describing. Without the segmenting fix, a learner who needs to re-watch compression must scrub a continuous timeline and overshoot. The walkthrough shows that the two principles solve different problems on the same frame: signaling routes attention in space, segmenting paces processing in time.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every cued element as a spending decision and ask what it cost. For each signal in the chapter's examples, name the element it makes more salient and the elements it necessarily makes less salient, because contrast is zero-sum. For each break, decide whether it sits on a seam or inside an idea. A strong reader can look at an over-busy frame and say not just \"too much going on\" but specifically \"five cues competing; the action only needs one; remove four.\"\n\nA strong reader of signaling and segmenting can state, for any frame, which single element earns the cue, which seam earns the break, and which learner the calibration is tuned to.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the trace documents restraint, not just application. It names at least one element the designer chose not to cue and one seam the designer chose not to break, with the reason, because demonstrating where you withheld a principle is stronger evidence of calibration than demonstrating where you applied it. The trace ties the calibration to a named learner (novice versus near-expert) and predicts how the cue count or segment count would change for the other.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"I reduced Frame 2 from four simultaneous step cues to one active-step cue and left the other headings plain, because contrast routes attention only if most of the frame stays quiet. I placed Continue controls at the three between-step seams and explicitly did not break mid-step, where the procedure is one continuous motion. This pacing targets first-time learners; for a refresher audience I would merge to two segments.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the calibration from a visual animation to an audio-first podcast lesson where there is no screen to cue. Signaling now lives in the voice: a deliberate pause, a change in pace, or the phrase \"here is the part that matters\" becomes the cue, and the same scarcity law holds, because if every sentence is emphasized vocally, none is. Segmenting becomes the chapter break or the prompt \"pause and try this before you continue.\" The transfer test is whether you can move both decisions into a channel with no visual contrast at all and still name the one cue and the one seam. If your signaling skill collapses the moment you lose arrows and color, you had learned the format, not the principle.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-02-signaling-and-segmenting-in-practice-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Signaling and Segmenting in Practice, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how signaling and segmenting in practice depends on evidence-based revision and bounded AI support to stay calibrated rather than over-applied.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice","stem":"A storyboard frame bolds the heading, boxes the diagram, colors three labels red, and adds a pulsing arrow, all at once. Why does this weaken signaling?","options":["Signaling works by contrast, so cuing almost everything leaves nothing standing out as essential.","Red is never an acceptable color for instructional cues.","Arrows can only be used in video, not still frames.","Bold headings always violate the coherence principle."],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Signaling directs limited attention. When many elements are cued at once, contrast collapses and the learner cannot tell what matters. Cut cues to the single essential element.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_more_signaling_is_better","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice","stem":"A designer says a frame was 'segmented' because the on-screen paragraph was split into two shorter ones, but the animation still plays straight through. What is missing for this to count as segmenting?","options":["A brighter font color on the second paragraph.","Learner control over pacing, such as a continue button or pause between the two meaningful units.","A third even shorter paragraph.","Background music under the animation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Segmenting gives the learner control over the pace of meaningful units. Shorter text inside a non-stop sequence is chunking the prose, not segmenting the experience.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_segmenting_is_short_text","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice","stem":"An AI tool suggests adding an animated glow around the key term to draw the eye. Before accepting, which check keeps the decision sound?","options":["Accept it, since motion always increases engagement.","Ask whether the glow's own motion adds processing that competes with reading the term it is meant to highlight.","Reject it because AI should never touch signaling decisions.","Accept it only if the glow matches the brand color."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A cue spends part of the load budget. An animated glow can demand more processing than the attention it saves, so check the cost against the benefit for the target learner.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_cue_adds_load","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice","stem":"You insert a pause in a four-step procedure simply because the segment felt long, landing the pause between step 2's setup and step 2's result. Why might this hurt learning?","options":["Pauses are never allowed inside a numbered procedure.","The boundary falls inside one conceptual unit, fragmenting the schema the learner is assembling for step 2.","Four steps is too many for any single frame.","The pause makes the total runtime shorter."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Segment boundaries belong at conceptual seams. Splitting in the middle of one idea interrupts schema construction, the opposite of what segmenting should do.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_segment_boundary_arbitrary","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_signaling_and_segmenting_in_practice"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_more_signaling_is_better","pattern":"signaling_as_quantity","description":"Believing more cues (bold, color, boxes, arrows) always improve learning, rather than that signaling works by directing limited attention to the essential element through contrast","trigger":"learner highlights most of the screen or stacks several cue types on one element","feedback":"Signaling works by contrast. When everything is cued, nothing is. Cut cues until only the essential element stands out.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_segmenting_is_short_text","pattern":"segmenting_confused_with_chunking","description":"Treating segmenting as merely splitting text into shorter paragraphs, rather than giving the learner control over the pace of meaningful units","trigger":"learner shortens paragraphs but keeps a single continuous auto-advancing animation","feedback":"Segmenting gives learner-paced units, for example a continue control or a pause. Shorter paragraphs in a non-stop sequence are not segmenting.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_cue_adds_load","pattern":"signaling_ignores_load_budget","description":"Adding a signaling cue without checking whether the cue itself adds extraneous processing for the target learner","trigger":"learner adds a motion or sound cue that competes with the content it is meant to highlight","feedback":"Check the load budget. A cue that itself demands processing can cost more than the attention it saves.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_2_segment_boundary_arbitrary","pattern":"segment_boundary_by_length","description":"Placing segment boundaries by length rather than at the seams between conceptual units","trigger":"learner inserts a pause mid-explanation because the segment was getting long","feedback":"Break at conceptual seams, not at arbitrary lengths. A pause inside one idea fragments the schema the learner is building.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"ea6e97b7f8f39120bad2bbf91f9d165c81a0d3275f34cf0f93e33f60976b13e0","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}